// The recurring three-beat block: split the problem, look each part up, connect
// it to something already built. Level 3, so the renderer withholds what each
// part DOES and asks the student to say it — the parts and their doc pointers
// are the level-3 bargain, and here the pointers do the work the code used to.
// Every reference points backwards: 9.1, and the pages that came before it.
const DECOMPOSE: DecomposeSpec = {
  big: 'Read one colour out of your own game — from the swatch in the Inspector, down to the four bytes it is really made of, and back again — without a converter open.',
  frame: 'concept',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Cut the hex string into bytes',
      does: 'Every two hex digits is exactly one byte, so `#FFE681` is three bytes and `#FFE681FF` is four. The `#` is a label, not part of the number.',
      doc: 'the Dec/Hex/Bin table in this lesson\'s Concept Lab, and the hex section of Binary & Hex',
      href: '../docs/unit-09/binary-and-hex.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Read one byte as a channel value',
      does: 'One hex digit covers four bits and two of them cover eight, so a channel runs 0 to 255: `e6` is 230, `81` is 129, and neither of them is a letter with a value.',
      doc: 'the eight-bits-stop-at-255 rule from 9.1, read against the same table',
      href: '9.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Say what the fourth byte is',
      does: 'Alpha: `ff` is solid, `80` is half-visible, `00` is invisible. A stored colour is four bytes rather than three because the web writes transparency somewhere else.',
      doc: 'the `Color32` paragraph of the Binary & Hex reference',
      href: '../docs/unit-09/binary-and-hex.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Count what the channels can express',
      does: '256 values per channel, three channels, so 256³ = 16,777,216 colours — and four bits per channel, as old hardware used, buys only 4,096.',
      doc: 'the colour-count socratic in this lesson\'s Concept Lab, argued from 9.1\'s bits-to-range law',
      href: '9.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Put the colour somewhere you can change it once',
      does: 'A colour named in one place and used everywhere is the exam\'s word for a data abstraction — one edit instead of forty, and the reason a palette is data rather than decoration.',
      doc: 'Step 2 of the Build Lab below, and the palette class it builds',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'The split is the whole lesson. "What colour is #FFE681?" is one question, and with a converter open it is a lookup; these five are five moves you can make on paper, and the last one is the move the exam grades as abstraction. A colour is not a picture and not a name — it is four numbers you chose, each with a range somebody else fixed at 0 to 255, and every colour bug in this unit is one of those four numbers being something you did not expect.',
  sizeTest:
    'The test: could you decode a colour with a pencil and the table on this page? If a step needs the eyedropper open, it is not part of reading a colour — it is part of finding one.',
  connect: [
    'In 9.1 you converted `health = 3` into eight bits. A colour channel is that same eight-bit number wearing a shorter coat: what is `e6` in decimal, and why can a channel never hold 256?',
    'In 6.2 you wrote `Gizmos.color = isGrounded ? Color.green : Color.red;` and named two colours instead of typing two triples. What did the name buy you there, and what would you have to look up in order to write that same green by hand?',
    'In 8.3 the tinker asks you to flash the boss\'s colour for a tenth of a second when a hit lands. If that flash is written as four numbers copied out of the Inspector, what breaks the day the boss is re-coloured — and which of the five parts above is the one that stops it?',
  ],
  turns: [
    'The coin you collect is drawn from `Coin_Animation.png`, whose busiest colour is `#FFE681`. Write it as three decimal bytes, then as a `Color32` call — and say which of those two spellings you would rather review in somebody else\'s code, and why.',
    'All three scenes in this project have the same camera background — the float triple (0.192, 0.302, 0.475) — because nobody ever changed Unity\'s default. Convert it to bytes, then to hex, then say which struct the scene file is actually storing and what changes if you move it into a `Color32`.',
  ],
};

const body = `<h1>9.2 — Hex, RGB &amp; Data Abstraction</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    You have clicked colors in Unity a hundred times. Behind that
    picker is the exact place where binary becomes USEFUL: colors are
    bytes, written in hex, and the hex is just binary with better
    handwriting. Today you read the Inspector's secret language.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I read a colour as a number?', 3, 'concept')}

${renderDecompose('9.2', DECOMPOSE, 3)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Concept Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Hex = binary in groups of 4.</strong> One hex digit
    (0–9, A–F) covers exactly 4 bits: 0000=0 … 1111=F. A byte is
    therefore exactly TWO hex digits. Nobody reads
    1111111111111111 when FF FF says the same thing out loud.</p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Dec</th><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>4</td><td>5</td><td>6</td><td>7</td><td>8</td><td>9</td><td>10</td><td>11</td><td>12</td><td>13</td><td>14</td><td>15</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Hex</th><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>4</td><td>5</td><td>6</td><td>7</td><td>8</td><td>9</td><td>A</td><td>B</td><td>C</td><td>D</td><td>E</td><td>F</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Bin</th><td>0000</td><td>0001</td><td>0010</td><td>0011</td><td>0100</td><td>0101</td><td>0110</td><td>0111</td><td>1000</td><td>1001</td><td>1010</td><td>1011</td><td>1100</td><td>1101</td><td>1110</td><td>1111</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p><strong>A color is four bytes.</strong> Unity's
    <code>Color32</code> is R, G, B, A — one byte each, 0–255, so a
    color is literally a hex number like <code>FFE681FF</code> (full
    red, a strong green, a little blue, full alpha). When you type
    <code>new Color32(255, 230, 129, 255)</code> you are writing
    <code>FFE681FF</code> in decimal cosplay. Here are three colours
    from your own project and from this page, decoded by hand:</p>
    <pre><code>// The coin you collect is drawn from Coin_Animation.png.
// Its busiest colour is #FFE681 → R=ff=255, G=e6=230, B=81=129

// The site you are reading right now is themed blue:
// #6cb2ff → R=6c=108, G=b2=178, B=ff=255  → (108, 178, 255)

// All three of your scenes still carry Unity's default camera
// background, because nobody ever chose anything else:
// (0.192, 0.302, 0.475) as floats → (49, 77, 121) as bytes → #314D79</code></pre>
    <p>That last one is worth a second look. The camera shows you a
    Color — four floats between 0 and 1 — and the machine's idea of
    the same colour is three bytes. Multiply each float by 255 and
    round: 0.192 × 255 = 49, which is <code>0x31</code>. Same colour,
    two spellings, and the byte one is the one you can count with.
    The # in CSS means "hex follows," and web colors are 6 hex digits
    because web alpha is written separately.</p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            The exam loves this: "how many colors can RGB represent?"
            Work it — 256 per channel, three channels — and say WHY
            the answer is 256³ (that is <a href="9.1.html">9.1</a>'s
            bits-to-range law, cubed). Then: why is 4 bits per
            channel (like old consoles) only 4096 colors — what did
            8-bit color BUY?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — decode your game's palette.</strong> Every
    Sprite Renderer in this project is still on its default tint —
    <code>(1, 1, 1, 1)</code>, which is <code>#FFFFFF</code> — so no
    object in the game is tinted from code. What you see on screen is
    the artwork and nothing else, which means the art IS the palette.
    Read three colours out of it with an eyedropper (the Sprite
    Editor has one, and so does any image editor), and write the hex
    AND the decimal triple for each:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>the stone ground tileset your cave walls are cut from →
            <code>#878579</code>, a desaturated grey</li>
        <li>the coin sheet, <code>Coin_Animation.png</code> →
            <code>#FFE681</code>, a bright gold</li>
        <li>the blood on the axe blade → <code>#8A2000</code>, a very
            dark red</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Then pick a fourth colour of your own and do the same. Notice
    what the hex made visible in one glance: the stone is high in all
    three channels and low in saturation, the gold is high in R and
    G with almost no B, and the blood is almost all R. Now you can
    QUOTE that instead of gesturing at it.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — program a palette.</strong> Colors as data —
    the abstraction, in code. These are the three you just read, with
    the byte values the eyedropper gave you:</p>
    <pre><code>public static class Palette
{
    //              what the eyedropper found    hex        Color32
    public static readonly Color32 CAVE_ROCK = new Color32(0x87, 0x85, 0x79, 0xff);
    public static readonly Color32 COIN_GOLD = new Color32(0xff, 0xe6, 0x81, 0xff);
    public static readonly Color32 BLOOD     = new Color32(0x8a, 0x20, 0x00, 0xff);
}

// the hit flash 8.3 asks you to build, written out:
spriteRenderer.color = new Color32(0xff, 0xff, 0xff, 0xff); // white for 0.1s
// phase 3 boss tint:
spriteRenderer.color = Palette.BLOOD;</code></pre>
    <p>Why a palette class beats per-object color picking: change ONE
    line, the whole game's mood shifts — and every object that was
    reading it changes at the same time, with no object-by-object
    hunting. That is <strong>abstraction</strong> — the exam's word,
    in your repo. It is also the reason the numbers above had to be
    read off the art rather than guessed: a palette is only worth
    anything if it is the same colour the artist drew.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — the alpha byte.</strong> The fourth byte is
    transparency. <code>0xff</code> = solid; <code>0x80</code> =
    half-visible (the hit flash above could EASE from 0x80 to 0xff
    instead of snapping). Every prefab in this project is currently
    <code>#FFFFFFFF</code> — fully opaque — so pick one and change
    only the fourth byte. <code>Turret_Projectile.prefab</code> is
    the clearest to watch: it draws <code>Turret_Bullet.png</code>,
    whose busiest colour is <code>#008DF0</code>, and dropping its
    alpha to <code>0x80</code> turns that solid blue bullet into a
    suggestion of one. Note the readability change: transparent
    threats read as "avoidable" — alpha is communication, not
    decoration.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Byte bugs</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>new Color32(300, 0, 0, 255) — read the constructor's
                parameter type and say why the compiler refuses the
                300 outright rather than storing it. Then: what would
                you have to change to store a value above 255, and
                why is a channel that cannot silently wrap a design
                win?</li>
            <li>0x1F vs 0xF1 — a transposed hex pair. What color
                difference did that make? Why is hex the FASTEST
                way to catch a digit-swap bug in colors?</li>
            <li>Compute: how many bytes in a 1080p frame of RGBA
                data? (1920×1080×4 — do it on paper. Then ask: why
                does your GPU do this 60 times a second? That
                arithmetic explains why <a href="9.7.html">9.7</a>
                needs a graphics pipeline.)</li>
            <li>The exam's classic: a 16-bit color system splits
                5/6/5. Why does GREEN get the extra bit — and why
                do human eyes barely notice (look at your palette:
                which channel did you lean on?)</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Palette design</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Design a DANGER palette shift for your boss phases:
                phase 1 neutral, phase 2 warm, phase 3 red-shifted.
                Write the three hex codes BEFORE you look at them —
                then check the feel.</li>
            <li>AP practice: "A game represents a color as 3 bits —
                one per channel. How many colors?" Answer AND
                reason — then tell your neighbor why old-school
                games looked "chunky."</li>
            <li>Hard: your own Color32 parser — read a string like
                "FFE681" with int.Parse(s, NumberStyles.HexNumber).
                Which two lines of code does this save in a
                data-driven palette file?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Convert hex ↔ binary ↔ decimal for bytes, fast</li>
            <li>Explain a color as four bytes and decode #RRGGBB by hand</li>
            <li>Build a palette class as a data abstraction</li>
            <li>Do the 256³ color-count argument from bits</li>
            <li>Break "read a colour as a number" into parts small enough to look each one up on its own, and say in one line what each part does</li>
            <li>Say which earlier lesson each part came from, and where you would reread it</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

